#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/id #uuid "bb373a5d-aa71-58ed-82e3-b74a8f51d144" :question/math-academy-id "e-114" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2.$ According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](images/02/02c989a33b6f027c0814d39d1468379dcb24f90536257b2b0bf4031191a73f85.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6).$\n\nSince $f(2)=3$ and $f(6)=11$, we must have \n$k\\in[3,11].$\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function, $f(x)=k$ may have solutions for $k < 3,$ but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d048aae2-5773-5da7-ab18-35d41bfbb53a" :question/math-academy-id "e-115" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/id #uuid "89750383-d5a6-5bf4-b6b2-abd489e44d3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-116" :question/problem "On which type of closed intervals can  the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\, ?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "e32fb82c-2e8f-5383-b15a-8f98aa86b716" :question/math-academy-id "e-6753" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c79e9567742a64066dfe42651f3a4e05847bd5c8b7d8ee616238010a2926c087.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-5)=1$ and $f(2)=4$, we must have $k\\in[1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b304347-eeba-582b-acf5-04fc708bf3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510802} {:answer/id #uuid "7808b15c-0db4-598e-9af1-b4b030735c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511030} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4f05e6-1064-5237-8152-301e601e93a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511104} {:answer/id #uuid "baa9601c-c987-51b0-8c57-a0a53c1b38c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511164} {:answer/id #uuid "c9dda439-ccd3-5069-8f04-60636b0d51d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b304347-eeba-582b-acf5-04fc708bf3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510802} :answer-field/id #uuid "31715bc6-dcf3-5721-ab14-4c1ca08aad86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60c91108-e44c-5a52-8d9f-b008dcd257b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105621" :question/problem "![](images/87/87bcea590bfb7357728d64cadabf4ab3e85135d312a60dd092ec546727a5fa30.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-1,4].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-1,4]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-1)$ and $f(4)$ inclusively.\n\nSince $f(-1)=5$ and $f(4)=-1$, we must have \n$k\\in [-1,5].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$-1$ and $5$\".\n\n![](images/1c/1c823d5f99ac9a126d3144197a493f347df20215c30b5ee7b28dc136459f00a2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a16361d-c7f3-5d0a-a9a2-83b038b0c4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511035} {:answer/id #uuid "a6595a77-7bd1-5d35-857f-6104adfa786e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511132} {:answer/id #uuid "cca99c35-d37a-5aac-91ef-5d9cf74bb486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511206} {:answer/id #uuid "db5d1df6-982e-58c9-bcf7-f4b59e4c781a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511234} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcab920-346d-5305-b97c-85f83eb95244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5d1df6-982e-58c9-bcf7-f4b59e4c781a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511234} :answer-field/id #uuid "4936f945-a8a7-583d-b9fa-25b703a244c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14a3bc41-5c72-5f24-9c07-376f58252a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-105670" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[-2,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=-4$ and $f(2)=0,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-4,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,0].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e518e9-b753-57d2-80df-fe41348834f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510776} {:answer/id #uuid "03808112-580d-56e4-b2a4-2317d31bbee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-6,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510784} {:answer/id #uuid "332285b0-e91b-57b0-a576-f6ca5cf0f2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510893} {:answer/id #uuid "79fcb8ae-30d6-5841-a620-17613af8e635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[3,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511033} {:answer/id #uuid "b454dec1-2be2-50f7-9bb7-ec9fac4d67c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-8,-4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b454dec1-2be2-50f7-9bb7-ec9fac4d67c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-8,-4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511156} :answer-field/id #uuid "417d21e0-0d55-5aa1-8095-733c8c96dbdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2715d4ad-4bbd-59ce-9226-02bf5ba9575d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105705" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-9}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-9=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=3.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=3.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-8,-4]$ is allowed. The intervals $(3,4)$ and $(-7,-3)$ are not closed, the interval $[-6,-1]$ contains the point $x=-3,$ while the interval $[3,5]$ contain the point $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dab59d9-8d5f-5743-b2ba-ac1d189d7f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510808} {:answer/id #uuid "13d6152b-91ad-53ff-8e73-d4dd221064be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510823} {:answer/id #uuid "869751bf-0fab-560d-b2e0-38fe5be8abba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511060} {:answer/id #uuid "b79dd5d8-dee2-5d68-8fd9-5ab751b2a6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511160} {:answer/id #uuid "c4532b91-ed3c-5f76-b794-27184920a48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dab59d9-8d5f-5743-b2ba-ac1d189d7f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510808} :answer-field/id #uuid "51eb9745-3497-568e-b210-1b5ebb30ffe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a118e6f-2768-508a-b130-7e64bda76208" :question/math-academy-id "q-105709" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-4x+4=0,$ which corresponds to $x=2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,1]$ is allowed. The intervals $(-2,1]$ and $[-1,2)$ are not closed, while $[0,2]$ and $[-1,3]$ contain the point $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f6a053c-5f1b-5cdf-8aca-963b7ac607b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238885} {:answer/id #uuid "76c89a10-b5c7-5e4d-af46-660055d3e634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-5,-3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239079} {:answer/id #uuid "829ab2da-3a5d-5bbb-9052-cd31b64cc806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-8,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239190} {:answer/id #uuid "c578ab7d-a573-5c0b-98db-a13e100e72e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-4,-2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239789} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb73c74-a9c9-5b6d-a630-7db64dffc37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb73c74-a9c9-5b6d-a630-7db64dffc37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240160} :answer-field/id #uuid "de800b12-b7a8-5928-99a5-4ec2e10ac47d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12db1f60-25f5-507f-bdcf-3245dfde49c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105710" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+5x+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}+5x+6=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,3]$ is allowed. The intervals $[-8,-3)$ and $(-2,1]$ are not closed, while $[-5,-3]$ and $[-4,-2]$ contain the point $x=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be33c5e4-2086-5d44-a684-333e21729caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be33c5e4-2086-5d44-a684-333e21729caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511177} :answer-field/id #uuid "543d11e1-6cdb-5661-b09d-c8b41c9f2d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75621f90-4584-51d0-a7ef-873c52eae724" :question/math-academy-id "q-344753" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-3,4].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-4$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $-1$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$\n\nThe equation $g(x)=1$ has at least\n{{field-1}} solutions." :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-3,4],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-3,4].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-3,4]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-2],$ we have $g(-3)=-4$ and $g(-2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [-4,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,-1],$ we have $g(-2)=3$ and $g(-1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=0$ and $g(1)=-2.$ Since $1\\notin [-2,0],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,3],$ we have $g(1)=-2$ and $g(3)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-2],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,4],$ we have $g(3)=-5$ and $g(4)=-1.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-3,-2]$ and $[-2,-1].$ \n\nTherefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least $2$ solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033434ee-d7b1-53a9-b9fc-d349d23e00e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "033434ee-d7b1-53a9-b9fc-d349d23e00e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315135} :answer-field/id #uuid "3664d67e-9a2f-5ba7-8e3c-e75c2710d2ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f368f3a-06db-598d-8b7b-f68ccf3faca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344761" :question/problem "The function $p(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-6,5].$ Some values of $p(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $p(x)=2?$\n\nThe equation $p(x)=2$ has at least {{field-1}} solutions." :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a continuous function on $[-6,5],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-6,5].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-6,5]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-6,-2],$ we have $p(-6)=4$ and $p(-2)=-1.$ Since $2\\in [-1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,5],$ we have $p(-2)=-1$ and $p(5)=-3.$ Since $2\\notin [-3,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $p(x)=2$ on $[-6,-2].$  \n\nTherefore, $p(x)=2$ has at least $1$ solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd58c2f-9a5c-56a0-b502-6fad91d539f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510919} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9ffd2c-dd45-507a-817e-8dcd915bedb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511078} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b03ebb-7afa-5de2-a056-ca74b06f0be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511223} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ad0ec0-5eeb-55bb-90c8-6a2b407c2776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511257} {:answer/id #uuid "f36c1b2f-f8a1-5e34-a62d-d5939d67d3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3ad0ec0-5eeb-55bb-90c8-6a2b407c2776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511257} :answer-field/id #uuid "17e5ed8e-738f-5c2f-abb0-b01d85dece0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b140ca1-518c-5294-933a-fe5b97f8ee4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47013" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+4.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [2,5]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,5],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(5).$\n\nSince $f(2)=8$ and $f(5)=29,$ we must have \n$k\\in [8,29].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=10$ belongs to the interval $[8,29].$\n\n![](images/14/14acd67d0b773864d39aaafcc6b41c9d8f3f10317b5e3f4584711baf157f346d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3397312b-322c-5838-add4-4c3ef92f9289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510894} {:answer/id #uuid "859cd724-e6e0-57c5-9905-18920a9c1447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511057} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb83e26-31fc-58df-adb4-932057c64208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511110} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f19c97-cdf6-5e14-aa26-e22cb035f7a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511131} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b29a61-0410-5720-bc50-a49bb3285d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0b29a61-0410-5720-bc50-a49bb3285d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-1,0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511248} :answer-field/id #uuid "cacf5e45-6f54-576f-b4d5-c0ab964d2657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7de45808-287e-58b6-b2fd-45e80ad721a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47019" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,2]$ and $[-1,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,2]$ and $[0,2]$ contain the point $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d71d7a5-84f6-5c9e-bb9e-45ee005734d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510876} {:answer/id #uuid "31dc79ee-ee5b-500d-a119-9ce1a09bd88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510887} {:answer/id #uuid "604be48b-5aad-5ab0-83bb-0f89ed9fb60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510987} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5cb47f-1426-50ff-b362-c08943c4bf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511106} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf793b6-7c2c-54c6-b01c-c14073dbc0c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31dc79ee-ee5b-500d-a119-9ce1a09bd88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510887} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0a4333-6056-5794-b8d1-b1934cbb02e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d898f3e-75a2-51d9-91a1-d58badf3f268" :question/math-academy-id "q-47053" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,8].$ Some values of $f(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $f(x)=-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[-1,8]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,8].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,8]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $f(-1)=-5$ and $f(0)=0.$ Since $-2\\in [-5,0],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,2],$ we have $f(0)=0$ and $f(2)=4.$ Since $-2\\notin [0,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,4],$ we have $f(2)=4$ and $f(4)=-1.$ Since $-2\\notin [-1,4],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[4,5],$ we have $f(4)=-1$ and $f(5)=-4.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,-1],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[5,8],$ we have $f(5)=-4$ and $f(8)=8.$ Since $-2\\in [-4,8],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0],$ $[4,5],$ and $[5,8].$ Therefore, $f(x)=-2$ has at least three solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f344881-385c-5665-85eb-bf009723e051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467572} {:answer/id #uuid "b0338624-0f00-542b-ad9e-7189e7be0ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f344881-385c-5665-85eb-bf009723e051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467572} :answer-field/id #uuid "267d92af-d407-5c35-ac8f-a6c162abe01d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468507} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0eccd15-4643-594c-9b76-cc1f8b6669b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467574} {:answer/id #uuid "acfa8399-1619-5e81-b743-8ccb518c5111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acfa8399-1619-5e81-b743-8ccb518c5111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467575} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6b3564-dbb4-56c7-a3c8-0f7852ea53a6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468508} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddc250d-3035-5ac8-aff3-71357621fce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467576} {:answer/id #uuid "57d257e8-63d5-5cd3-990e-9061ae7ffc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ddc250d-3035-5ac8-aff3-71357621fce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467576} :answer-field/id #uuid "054c1693-cd72-59a0-8e2f-778669cf35f6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee052f2c-7f8b-5e1c-a710-1b1e4c2963cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-47055" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n- **I.** $[-4,-1]$\n- **II.** $[3,5]$\n- **III.** $[-1,4]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a6dd30-b257-5143-908c-94806b79918b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327675} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a11625-83a7-523d-aa62-5f659127b27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328466} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4cccbe-c232-5969-94c3-cc1c0827b4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328585} {:answer/id #uuid "eb627d11-6b89-5be3-8bb0-8db63422debb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328993} {:answer/id #uuid "f85501cf-6174-511f-b788-63273ac0be7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f85501cf-6174-511f-b788-63273ac0be7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329111} :answer-field/id #uuid "6551fdd8-34e7-54c6-aa84-f41b0b354cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dd8dfeb-1c7c-5f8b-910b-7d58a3bb506e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47056" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-4,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-4,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-4,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-4,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[-4,-3],$ we have $g(-4)=2$ and $g(-3)=-1.$ Since $1\\in [-1,2],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-1],$ we have $g(-3)=-1$ and $g(-1)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $g(-1)=-5$ and $g(0)=2.$ Since $1\\in [-5,2],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,1],$ we have $g(0)=2$ and $g(1)=3.$ Since $1\\notin [2,3],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=3$ and $g(2)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-4,-3],$ $[-1,0],$  and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least three solutions on $[-4,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11904119-a193-52e5-a726-77435d9078cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327086} {:answer/id #uuid "41a35ce7-fd1e-5a3c-98e9-33226a993fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327511} {:answer/id #uuid "5889f12a-e473-54e7-998d-9fe3253e5ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327709} {:answer/id #uuid "8cce18a1-ac0d-5ee0-81d8-59ec1211d61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328144} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0487d1-7626-5782-bf7f-dc13ed94d563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5889f12a-e473-54e7-998d-9fe3253e5ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327709} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f8a1662-58bc-58f6-bb48-4c6b4004daa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f019924-a46f-5bc3-be22-35c5680cdf03" :question/math-academy-id "q-47058" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-4.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-4,-2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,-2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-4)$ and $f(-2).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=5$ and $f(-2)=-3,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-3,5].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=2$ belongs to the interval $[-3,5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0224e1ad-7d28-51e0-b5be-99fdaf9f354d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510779} {:answer/id #uuid "0da21742-bc6f-5a5c-949b-2c60da707f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510807} {:answer/id #uuid "46cb9080-7932-5380-a15f-5c65924138ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510942} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b2d814-242e-5411-8cbb-8d9b3d6ebce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511182} {:answer/id #uuid "d21efb8c-0299-51f7-b5d9-24f0b2b1ed5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0b2d814-242e-5411-8cbb-8d9b3d6ebce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511182} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd25d2f7-fa41-5617-b9af-782c028c8030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c198801-a87e-5360-90a9-0643872362f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47084" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-1)(x+1)(x-2).$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,3],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(3).$\n\nSince $f(0)=2$ and $f(3)=8,$ we must have \n$k\\in [2,8].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=0$ does not belong to the interval $[2,8].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73456ba9-56d5-5c2f-9bb3-0f255baaa1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511022} {:answer/id #uuid "95ef29c0-8c06-5acf-9af0-bffc2f268169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511093} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c42683-7d2e-56d1-aab0-70703cd960e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511157} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c6c514-c088-5630-96e3-d9f2191a0659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511262} {:answer/id #uuid "f1aa6b76-69df-564d-b084-f23ee24ba4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95ef29c0-8c06-5acf-9af0-bffc2f268169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511093} :answer-field/id #uuid "523da1f1-a357-58c6-b31e-b24677539075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "649e731a-c63d-5be5-bf6d-a7054ad65b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-48342" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x}$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[0,4].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [0,4]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,4],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(4).$\n\nSince $f(0)=0$ and $f(4)=14$, we must have \n$k\\in [0,14].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$0$ and $14$\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e50f37f-de04-5f77-ba2f-0ee2ac1b3b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132895} {:answer/id #uuid "e97b8ed1-34bd-5695-ad22-e12a6cc61fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III, II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132896} {:answer/id #uuid "fa53d2ea-c1bd-5aa5-965f-9513e9eaafc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132897} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7c910c-3f13-51d6-bee7-ce0c300dfe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132898} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9192d3-9f25-57bd-a508-793119bd9a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e50f37f-de04-5f77-ba2f-0ee2ac1b3b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132895} :answer-field/id #uuid "16a5fed7-59bc-5485-b040-90f121190eda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69dfc1c5-3195-5cf2-bc4a-740b775a4bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-48343" :question/problem "A statement of the intermediate value theorem is given below but some of its parts are missing.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is $\\text{ ______ }$ on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ _________ }$, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nGoing from left to right, in which order should the parts I-III listed below be placed into the blank spaces to complete the statement of the theorem?\n\nI. increasing\n\nII. continuous\n\nIII. $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ inclusively" :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem states the following.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is **continuous** on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(a)$ **and** $f(b)$ **inclusively**, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d232c74-e493-5e95-b19e-bef59d74487c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in [-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510848} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb9c562-97a9-59a7-9eae-91d8984e88a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in [-2,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510873} {:answer/id #uuid "93a5119a-6a61-5673-8be7-e0b3955b04c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in (-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511085} {:answer/id #uuid "b37e21ab-0f08-5606-90dc-a04d243f4e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in (-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511152} {:answer/id #uuid "eb407e1a-f60c-5528-b589-8ea53f4c6814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in [-1,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cb9c562-97a9-59a7-9eae-91d8984e88a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c\\in [-2,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510873} :answer-field/id #uuid "3259bb32-160b-584a-9d3c-ccfd3a1756c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc22bd7d-9c0b-50f8-80e8-b40ec1e8460f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48358" :question/problem "![](images/de/dededc3b9924ff278f45a6149a0b7a5ca0464ee2c3b80aa85d12187a86a93725.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[-2,3].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $-1$ and $1$ inclusively, then there exists a number $\\text{ ________ }$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,3]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(3)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,3]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(3)=-1.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,3]$\"\n\n![](images/85/85a590ba283dae96329692fee1aa6e08045fb06e5baa8a42d8308fe8c2558f02.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10486862-eea8-5f24-aa4a-d6d2fbd26b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510812} {:answer/id #uuid "1b619c48-3bce-5e1d-8b28-fcf815ae2d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510845} {:answer/id #uuid "58043564-3e8f-5ae4-9d82-310f00ae830c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510969} {:answer/id #uuid "5e51eed2-2e72-557f-9004-4df908115a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510982} {:answer/id #uuid "c62caf40-f560-5422-bda2-43d53eb6ef4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b619c48-3bce-5e1d-8b28-fcf815ae2d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510845} :answer-field/id #uuid "be017598-1e24-5bf6-b57e-7e6e33872bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "889fc732-d136-5234-a89b-7831dcd5eacb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48425" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ which is defined and continuous on the closed interval $[0,8].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [0,8]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,8],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(0)$ and $f(8).$\n\nSince $f(0)=0$ and $f(8)=2$, we must have \n$k\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$0$ and $2$\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4467b80f-ae50-5444-a167-60efa89eaef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345941} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5511ff-7d5c-5a1f-845a-ce67bfc8d306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346303} {:answer/id #uuid "9e66446d-348d-5a6a-9a83-40552ce32d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346732} {:answer/id #uuid "c463a5ea-6e6c-5c44-ae22-48ca75bf8431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347047} {:answer/id #uuid "de1cdaea-8c75-5e83-b7fd-f2cf0701f75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e66446d-348d-5a6a-9a83-40552ce32d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346732} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5f8dae9-4d09-5215-9e12-2d4e29684707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03e4b68b-1dcd-579e-9183-2b2d2ce44e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-78510" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=8-(x+2)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-2,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=8$ and $f(1)=-1,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-1,8].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=5$ belongs to the interval $[-1,8].$\n\n![](images/4b/4b9b0cecb305a49f98b7b7a6a2ebca69c1ce10acdb72cb88d805e0f1901dc882.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a513028-7494-52ef-ad77-c476ad1110db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345582} {:answer/id #uuid "4f653602-ed65-5294-8d6c-a6ef40f95a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346030} {:answer/id #uuid "5f729d70-c219-5012-b6a4-33000a396e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346175} {:answer/id #uuid "7de2999b-8a5b-506d-ad8d-ee40e381570c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346453} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ab31d1-2bed-5320-91a1-aa82ac71d5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de2999b-8a5b-506d-ad8d-ee40e381570c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346453} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a7db5c-f3b0-55a6-bffd-8b68714c8c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae2fb37c-3d07-5853-b99d-9e361dcd461b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78542" :question/problem "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-1,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,2].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=2$ and $g(1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,2],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=0$ and $g(2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT  *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-1,1]$ and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least two solutions."}]}
